ct you  personally based on your genetic makeup  and so uh this is a become a very very  controversial uh practice um but  interestingly and also uh has received a  lot of attention in the in the lay press  and in fact in 2008  time magazine named the retail dna test  in particular siding 23 and these tests  as one of the the best inventions of  2008  and uh but you know as i as i kind of  alluded to this is this has been very  controversial  so um  you know certainly in scientific  literature we've seen many many many  opinion pieces and commentaries that  have uh you know kind of sharply  criticized this practice so genomic  profiles for disease risk because it's  predictive or premature  you know do you know kind of hinting at  consumer protection issues by asking do  you know what you're buying  also hinting at this issue that  potentially offering information like  this can can be harmful to people um and  that uh you know  perhaps because of this um you know it  should be offered in a different way  perhaps through a healthcare provider or  you know potentially not offered at all  um so these are some of the issues that  that have arisen  and so uh you know this is just to uh to  uh kind of even further underscore that  certainly in europe there have been you  know also  in some ways kind of in parallel into  the u.s uh discussions about  how to regulate this type of testing and  in some instances um  certain countries have taken an even  more conservative approach uh for  example  a number of years ago germany actually  banned  receiving testing directly to the  consumer and  in fact  mandated that it be provided  through through a healthcare  intermediary  okay so just to kind of make explicit  what are the arguments for and against  this type of testing  so  essentially um you know and i just want  to kind of touch on some of the  highlights here  people who are in favor of direct  consumer testing especially have an  argument around uh you know sort of  in