ions are still  underway um policies or policy-making is  still ongoing  but it's  kind of an interesting area to sort of  watch  but this notion of  requiring a healthcare intermediary is  also one that i think deserves um uh you  know significant attention  um there's been a lot of discussion  about whether or not uh physicians are  sort of as a group are sort of the right  group um to be the ones to sort of  be the translators of this information  for patients for consumers  and an interesting uh survey was  published uh by medco a couple of years  ago where they surveyed over 10 000  physicians and  interestingly they found that um you  know 98 of physicians said that they  didn't doubt that genomics does  influence uh drug response for instance  so pharmacogenomics  but uh strikingly only one out of ten  reported that they thought they had  adequate knowledge of pharmacogenomics  to actually utilize that information in  their practice so  you know and a lot of other things have  been written about this particular issue  sort of uh really questioning the extent  to which um  your physicians as a group have the  uh  sort of enough training at this point in  time  um  to to be the identified translators  and so if not physicians i mean one can  ask the question well who who should be  the translators and so an obvious group  to consider are certainly the genetic  counselors uh one issue there is just  the lack of there's such a small number  of genetic counselors in the us um you  know less than three thousand in fact um  which is one uh per 135 000 people in  the us so with the dramatically  increasing availability of genomic  information  um  uh  it just seems um you know sort of  untenable to uh to to the idea that a  genetic counselor would be involved  or even a medical geneticist since there  are very few of those professionals as  well  that would have to be involved in every  single kind of clinical  issue centered around genomics  and in addition i think 