s  a very important role for physicians and  possibly other providers uh sort of in  this process of directive consumer  testing  but um i think you know for that to  really um  sort of resonate um i think there's a  real need for greater positioning  education about genomics  to kind of close this delivery gap  and just once again i would just say  that  there's really a strong need for  additional research um  our studies i think have provided some  initial insights into this area but have  also raised new questions  so  i look forward to seeing  new work in this space  and just want to acknowledge my  my  collaborators and also  the funding agencies for this work  thank you very much  and i'll take questions now  okay  i apologize it took me a minute to find  the questions  um  please just bear with me a moment while  i try to read some of them  okay so i see one question here  and i'll just read the question briefly  speaking about direct to consumer from a  regulation perspective the bottleneck is  the lack of proper certification for the  technology being used  it is only recently that illumina  i guess they have applied for fda  approval of their sequencers  um  so uh  certainly there's  there's a lot going on in the regulatory  space  around um sort of a certification um  for the different aspects of sort of the  testing pipeline  the sequencers  the analysis algorithms the reports  themselves um and in fact uh  23andme applied to the fda i believe  just a month or so ago  um to get uh certain aspects of their  tests  uh approved um through through the fda  so um  i'm not certain uh what the question is  here but i would say that i think that  there's a lot that's going on in this  space and i think um you know it'll be  interesting in the coming months to see  how the fda responds um especially to  23andme's application  um  uh you know i think it's it's it's hard  to uh  imagine that there they would you know  sort of be shut down or something like  that  