ny  uh produces estimates that are  necessarily more clinically valid i  think we would need  very large longitudinal studies to  really to really uh  know whether that was the case or not  we actually did do an offshoot study of  the sghi  where we looked at the extent to which  the risk estimates provided were  associated with people's  reports of what their family history was  and what their own personal history of  disease was and  um we actually found that uh  you know um  there were significant statistically  significant associations suggesting um  you know that that that on some level  the test was kind of um you know giving  information that was consistent with  people's backgrounds but  um you know the effects were quite small  and so hopefully i see another question  here that says any info on the  reliability and quality of this testing  so i hope that that the answer i just  gave  will hopefully get at that question as  well  okay so i'm seeing another question here  people can hurt themselves with many  types of information  many of which have remained unregulated  so how is genetic information different  than say someone's credit report i think  that's a great question um for the  extent to which information can be  harmful especially if it gets in you  know into the wrong hands  um  so i think uh  yeah and into to the extent that that um  you know i think that there could be i  think what what  what  possibly this question the crux of it  kind of gets that is the issue of um you  know sort of discrimination based on  genetic information and  you know it's certainly the case that  uh genetic discrimination laws um have  have not been able to kind of keep pace  with you know the the rapidly advancing  technology and the event the wide  availability of genetic information  um  but you know there there is the gina  legislation um certain states have  passed legislation that uh it is  actually even more protective of  people's kind of genetic privacy and  